<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <!-- Phase 1532 — NOT PACKABLE. This project is a console `Exe` whose `main` is
         a synthetic demo: it fabricates telemetry records, runs the detectors over
         them and prints the result. That is a manual-run diagnostic, and it was
         being packed and published to nuget.org as `Fuaran.UI.Telemetry.Drift` —
         so the public feed carried a package whose entry point analyses data it
         invented, beside packages that are libraries a consumer composes.
         Nothing about the detector modules changes: they stay where they are, and
         the telemetry test suite still project-references them. What stops is the
         PUBLICATION. Re-packing this is a deliberate act that has to answer what
         the `Exe` is for on a consumer's machine, and the honest answer today is
         that it is for nothing there. -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- Phase 1648 — the package metadata below is INERT and kept deliberately, not
         because it is still true of a shipped package. `IsPackable=false` means nothing
         reads it, and `Build.fs` no longer names this project in `packableProjects`, so
         there is no path by which these strings reach a feed. They stay because they are
         the accurate description of what the modules in here do, and deleting them would
         lose that rather than clarify it; the first line says what the reader needs to
         know, which is that this is not published. Re-packing is the deliberate act the
         comment above describes, and it starts by answering what the `Exe` is for on a
         consumer's machine. -->
    <PackageId>Fuaran.UI.Telemetry.Drift</PackageId>
    <Description>NOT PUBLISHED (IsPackable=false since Phase 1532). Drift detector for Fuaran op-apply telemetry. Computes per-OpKind aggregate metrics (success rate, p50/p95 latency, top decoder-rejection reasons, top NodeNotFound NodeIds) and flags week-on-week success-rate regressions exceeding an operator-tunable threshold (default 10pp). Also ships a deterministic StyleFlag drift detector (Phase 151) — the resolved-style twin that diffs declared-invariant flag sets across two windows of StyleObservation to surface newly-introduced / cleared visual-legibility violations, severity-weighted by the ThemeManifest's invariant weights (semantic visual regression, no pixels, CI-gateable). Manual-run console tool for now (`dotnet run --project src/Fuaran.UI.Telemetry.Drift`); cron-friendly later when an operator-decided threshold is wired into a scheduled job. Companion to Fuaran.UI.Telemetry.Abstractions. Apache-2.0 licensed.</Description>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageTags>fuaran;telemetry;drift;observability;regression</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="Defaults.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Aggregates.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Detect.fs" />
    <Compile Include="ProviderDrift.fs" />
    <Compile Include="StyleDrift.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Program.fs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Fuaran.UI.Telemetry.Abstractions\Fuaran.UI.Telemetry.Abstractions.fsproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Fuaran.UI.StyleObserver.Abstractions\Fuaran.UI.StyleObserver.Abstractions.fsproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Fuaran.UI.ThemeManifest\Fuaran.UI.ThemeManifest.fsproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="FSharp.Core" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
